Q6 – Priority, Order and Fairness in a Queue (8 Marks)
1. Why a normal Queue is not sufficient
A normal Queue follows FIFO (First In, First Out). This means the first patient who arrives is treated first, regardless of their medical condition.

However, hospital triage must follow two rules:
1. The patient with the lowest severity number must be treated first (Severity 1 is most critical).
2. If two patients have the same severity, the patient who arrived earlier must be treated first.
Therefore, a normal FIFO queue is not suitable. A PriorityQueue with a custom Comparator is more appropriate because it orders patients according to severity and arrival time.
2. Complete Java program
Save the file as Triage.java.

import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Patient {
    final String name;
    final int severity;
    final long arrival;

    Patient(String name, int severity, long arrival) {
        this.name = name;
        this.severity = severity;
        this.arrival = arrival;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + "(S" + severity + ")";
    }
}

public class Triage {
    private long counter = 0;

    // Lower severity number gets higher priority.
    // If severity is equal, earlier arrival gets higher priority.
    private final PriorityQueue<Patient> queue =
        new PriorityQueue<>(
            Comparator.comparingInt((Patient p) -> p.severity)
                      .thenComparingLong(p -> p.arrival)
        );

    void admit(String name, int severity) {
        queue.add(new Patient(name, severity, counter++));
    }

    Patient next() {
        return queue.poll();
    }

    // Display patients in actual treatment order without
    // removing them from the original queue.
    void displayWaitingList() {
        PriorityQueue<Patient> copy = new PriorityQueue<>(queue);

        System.out.println("Waiting list:");
        while (!copy.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.print(copy.poll() + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Triage er = new Triage();

        er.admit("Ravi", 3);
        er.admit("Meena", 1);
        er.admit("John", 3);
        er.admit("Asha", 5);
        er.admit("Kumar", 1);
        er.admit("Lata", 2);

        er.displayWaitingList();

        StringBuilder order = new StringBuilder();
        Patient p;

        while ((p = er.next()) != null) {
            order.append(p).append(" ");
        }

        System.out.println("Treatment order:");
        System.out.println(order.toString().trim());
    }
}



Expected output:
Waiting list:
Meena(S1) Kumar(S1) Lata(S2) Ravi(S3) John(S3) Asha(S5)
Treatment order:
Meena(S1) Kumar(S1) Lata(S2) Ravi(S3) John(S3) Asha(S5)


3. How the Comparator and arrival counter work
The Comparator has two conditions:
Comparator.comparingInt((Patient p) -> p.severity)
          .thenComparingLong(p -> p.arrival)


- comparingInt() sorts patients by severity in ascending order. Severity 1 comes before severity 2, 3, and so on.
- thenComparingLong() compares arrival numbers when two patients have the same severity. The smaller arrival number comes first.
The counter is increased every time a patient is admitted:
queue.add(new Patient(name, severity, counter++));


For example:
Patient	Severity	Arrival counter
Ravi	3	0
Meena	1	1
John	3	2
Asha	5	3
Kumar	1	4
Lata	2	5

Meena and Kumar both have severity 1. Since Meena's arrival counter is 1 and Kumar's is 4, Meena is treated first. Similarly, Ravi is treated before John because both have severity 3.
