5. Growth investigation – Time complexity
Before testing, we can predict the cost of each operation.
Operation	PriorityQueue complexity	Explanation
Admit a patient (add)	O(log n)	The heap may need to rearrange elements to maintain priority order.
Serve next patient (poll)	O(log n)	The root is removed and the heap is rearranged.
View next patient (peek)	O(1)	The highest-priority patient is at the root.
Store all patients	O(n)	Each patient occupies one queue entry.

Here, n represents the number of waiting patients.

Test with three meaningful queue sizes:
- 1,000 patients
- 10,000 patients
- 100,000 patients
You can use the following benchmark method in the same Triage class to measure admission and removal. Call benchmark(); at the end of main() if you want the program to print the timings too.

static void benchmark() {
    int[] sizes = {1000, 10000, 100000};

    System.out.println("\nPriorityQueue benchmark");
    System.out.println("Patients\tAdd time (ms)\tRemove time (ms)");

    for (int n : sizes) {
        PriorityQueue<Patient> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(
            Comparator.comparingInt((Patient p) -> p.severity)
                      .thenComparingLong(p -> p.arrival)
        );

        long startAdd = System.nanoTime();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            pq.add(new Patient("Patient" + i, (i % 5) + 1, i));
        }

        long endAdd = System.nanoTime();

        long startRemove = System.nanoTime();

        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            pq.poll();
        }

        long endRemove = System.nanoTime();

        double addTime = (endAdd - startAdd) / 1_000_000.0;
        double removeTime = (endRemove - startRemove) / 1_000_000.0;

        System.out.printf("%d\t\t%.3f\t\t%.3f%n",
                          n, addTime, removeTime);
    }
}
